# RROWM semantic signal rule

UI signals must map to **registry meaning**, not generic UI state.

Do **not** use colors primarily to indicate arbitrary component state (hover, selected tab, “info” badge, etc.). Color is part of the **registry language**.

## Canonical mappings

| Event | Signal |
|-------|--------|
| Registration | cobalt |
| Valuation | violet / cool signal |
| Sale | ember |
| Transfer / stewardship | lime |
| Certification / verification | monochrome seal |
| Correction / exception | amber |

A transfer should feel like a transfer everywhere. A sale should feel like a sale everywhere.

## Must stay consistent across

Chronology entries · event stamps · CTAs (when event-specific) · badges · notifications · modal headers · ledger surfaces · certificate markers

## Implementation

- **Source of truth:** `lib/registry-semantic-signals.ts` (`semanticSignalBarClass`, `semanticStampClass`, `semanticDotClass`, `notificationSemanticEvent`)
- **Chronology / ownership:** `lib/registry-event-visual.ts` (maps narrative kinds → semantic events)
- **Acquisition filing timeline:** `lib/acquisition-filing-visual.ts` (step → semantic event for execution UI)
- **Semantic motion:** `styles/semantic-motion.ts` (event → motion preset; CSS + Framer)
- **Consequence feedback:** `styles/consequence-feedback.ts` (consequential action acknowledgement)
- **CSS tokens:** `app/globals.css` (`--v2-*-signal`, `.v2-signal-bar--*`, `.registry-event-stamp--*`)
- **Primitives:** `styles/rrowm-v2.ts` (`v2Signal`)

When adding UI accent color, ask: *which registry event does this represent?* If none, use neutral paper/graphite — not a semantic signal.
